## ABSTRACT
As the most promising alternative to traditional indium tin oxide (ITO), silver nanowire (AgNW) composite transparent electrodes with improved stabilities compared with that of the pristine AgNWs networks have been demonstrated in various devices. However, a stable AgNW/polymer composite as the bottom electrode for perovskite solar cells has not yet been reported. Here, a long-term stable, smooth $\mathrm{AgNW}$ composite with an antioxidant-modified chitosan polymer was developed. The modified polymer can effectively protect pristine AgNWs from side reactions with perovskite, whereas it does not block the carrier drift through the interface of the insulating polymer. The as-prepared AgNW/polymer composite electrode exhibited a root mean square roughness below $10\mathrm{nm}$ at a scan size of $50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\times50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}},$ and its original sheet resistance did not change obviously after aging at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 40 days in air. As a result, the perovskite solar cell employing the composite as the bottom electrode yielded a power conversion efficiency of $7.9\%$ , which corresponds to nearly $75\%$ of that of the reference device with an ITO electrode.
## 1 Introduction
Transparent electrodes (TEs) are considered essential components for many optoelectronic applications [1–7]. At present, the most widely used TEs are made from doped metal oxides, especially indium tin oxide (ITO), which has excellent opto-electrical performance $(<10\Omega\cdot\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ at $90\%$ transmittance). However, grain growth in ITO coatings on polymer substrates is difficult because the annealing temperature must be kept low due to the limited thermal stability of polymer substrates. This limited grain growth results in a lower conductive performance $\left(\sim35\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}\right.$ at $90\%$ transmittance). Moreover, preparing ITO using a non-continuous sputtering process is much more expensive than roll-to-roll solution-processed manufacturing, and ITO’s brittleness is a disadvantage for fabricating flexible devices. These inherent drawbacks have encouraged the development of alternative materials for the next generation of TEs. Silver nanowires (AgNWs) are among the most promising alternatives because they offer excellent flexibility, good conductivity, and easy solution-based processing [8–14].
Recently, AgNWs have been demonstrated in applications as bottom or top TEs in polymer solar cells, dye-sensitized solar cells, and copper indium gallium diselenide and silicon solar cells [15–21]. Hence, there has been an expectation that AgNWs would replace ITO in perovskite solar cells. However, because of their crucial stability issue, employing AgNWs in perovskite solar cells remains a great challenge. During perovskite precursor solution coating and perovskite crystal growth, damage to AgNWs occurs due to side reactions between the perovskite and silver. Thus, numerous attempts to develop composite AgNW electrodes have been reported to achieve better stability for high-performance ITO-free devices [22–26].
Thus far, a AgNW composite has been successfully demonstrated as the bottom electrode for a perovskite solar cell by applying conductive metal oxides on top of the $\mathtt{A g N W}$ networks to protect the AgNWs. For example, $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ of fluorine-doped $Z\mathrm{nO}$ (FZO) was pulsed-laser deposited onto the $\mathtt{A g N W}$ networks, resulting in a composite electrode with a transmittance of $83\%$ at a sheet resistance of $17\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1},$ , along with a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $3.29\%$ for the corresponding perovskite solar cell [27]. A sandwichtype ITO/AgNW/ITO composite was fabricated via solution processing and post-annealing at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for the bottom spin-coated ITO layer for 30 min in a $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}$ atmosphere and at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for the upper ITO layer. The composite exhibited a transmittance of $72.5\%$ and a sheet resistance of $\left.18\Omega\cdot\mathrm{sq}^{-1}\right..$ , resulting in a perovskite solar cell with a PCE of $8.44\%$ [28]. This represented significant progress in the incorporation of AgNW composites into perovskite solar cells. However, adding the metal oxide seriously reduced the transmittance of $\mathtt{A g N W}$ electrode, and thermally annealing at a temperature that is too high, even under a reducing atmosphere, could potentially damage the AgNWs. Nonetheless, this annealing step is required to achieve good conductivity for sol–gel ITO films, further complicating the process.
There have been also several attempts to combine AgNWs with conductive polymers or carbon materials. For example, AgNW composites with a conductive poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(styrenesulfon ate) (PEDOT:PSS) layer are most frequently investigated for applications in polymer solar cells, etc., revealing a good conductive performance [15, 29–31]. However, these composites have been found to exhibit poor long-term stability, and their sheet resistance started to increase after only $40\mathrm{~h~}$ of aging at low humidity $(\sim45\%$ relative humidity) and room temperature. Thus far, no successful application of this type of composite has been demonstrated in perovskite solar cells. Another widely investigated composite electrode is AgNW/graphene or graphene oxide (GO), which has been used in various solar cells, organic light-emitting diodes (OLEDs), etc. [32–38]. A AgNW/GO composite was reported to maintain its original resistance after 8 days of aging at $85^{\circ}C$ [36], and a chemical-vapordeposited graphene/AgNW/polycarbonate composited was stable for 30 days at room temperature [39], revealing a $\mathtt{A g N W}$ composite with improved stability. However, stability tests under harsher conditions such as long-term aging at elevated temperature have rarely been reported, and no applications of $\mathrm{AgNW/}$ graphene in perovskite solar cells have been successfully demonstrated. Notably, several kinds of AgNW composites have been reported with traditional insulating polymers such as teflon and poly(ether sulfone) (PES), which enhance the composite stability well. However, they have rarely been applied in solar cells or OLEDs, because carriers in the active layer are possibly blocked during movement to the bottom electrode due to the interlayer polymer barrier, resulting in very small current densities [40, 41]. Thus, developing highperformance AgNW composite electrodes with longterm stability via a simple solution-processing approach while enabling application in perovskite solar cells as the bottom electrode is still a great challenge.
In this work, a new kind of AgNW composite electrode with an antioxidant-modified biopolymer was developed using a solution process at low temperature. Instead of the previously reported conductive metal oxide, a nanoscale insulating layer of antioxidantmodified chitosan was added. The as-prepared electrode combines the advantages of the above-mentioned composites while demonstrating additional strengths. It shows good long-term stability with an almost unchanged sheet resistance after 40 days of aging in air at an elevated temperature of $85^{\circ}C,$ and its sheet resistance decreased by $81.6\%$ from its maximum at 110 to $20.2\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ after adding the polymer. Furthermore, its surface roughness was significantly improved, with a root mean square (RMS) roughness $<10\mathrm{nm}$ over a large scan size of $50\upmu\mathrm{m}\times50\upmu\mathrm{m},$ which helps to minimize short circuits in high-performance devices, especially solar cells. Most importantly, an AgNW composite with all the desired features for solar cells can be realized based on an extremely thin overcoating polymer that allows charge carriers to pass through. Therefore, the AgNW/chitosan composite was successfully incorporated into perovskite solar cells as the bottom electrode, yielding a PCE of $7.9\%,$ which is comparable to that of a reference device with an ITO electrode.
## 2 Results and discussion
Figure 1 is a schematic diagram of the AgNW composite electrode synthesis. The biopolymer chitosan containing an amino group on every single unit was first mixed with the antioxidant acid in water to form a colorless, clear, and relatively viscous solution. Then, the solution was deposited on the as-prepared AgNW networks with glass or poly(ethylene naphthalate) (PEN) as the substrate, resulting in a AgNW composite electrode with a nanoscale top layer of the chitosan complex.
In this work, three antioxidant acids were employed to modify the chitosan, including citric acid (CiA), tartaric acid (TA) and ascorbic acid (AsA) [42]. A clear solution could be obtained by adding ascorbic acid to chitosan at a molar ratio of 1:1, whereas much more acid (5:1) than the stoichiometric amount was required when citric acid or tartaric acid was used, probably resulting from the strong internal binding strength between multiple acid groups. As shown in Fig. 2, the thermal stabilities of the pristine $\mathtt{A g N W}$ networks and the various AgNW composites were investigated. The pristine AgNWs maintained their original shapes after annealing at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 2(a)), but small breaks along the AgNWs occurred in the following anneal at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 2(b)), and fusing where AgNWs crossed was found as the annealing temperature increased to $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 2(c)). In contrast, the morphology of the AgNW composite with citricacid-modified chitosan $(\mathrm{AgNW/Chi\mathrm{-}C i A)}$ did not obviously change after annealing at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 2(d)) but was completely damaged during the following anneal at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figs. 2(e) and 2(f)). Some AgNWs were broken into several distinct pieces, and others degraded into globules. The AgNWs in the AgNW composite with tartaric-acid-modified chitosan (AgNW/ Chi-TA) similarly degraded. Furthermore, small agglomerations formed on the AgNW networks when Chi-TA was used, resulting in a serious decrease in transmittance, but they disappeared after annealing at $140^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figs. $2(\mathrm{g}){-}2(\mathrm{i}))$ . In sharp contrast, the AgNW composite with ascorbic-acid-modified chitosan (AgNW/ Chi-AsA) was considerably more stable during thermal annealing (Figs. 2(j)–2(l)). Even when ascorbic acid was added at the same ratio to chitosan as citric acid and tartaric acid (5:1), the AgNWs maintained their original shape without any degradation after consecutive anneals at 110, 140 and $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ at each temperature (Fig. 2(j)). This suggests the possible long-term stability of AgNWs with Chi-AsA. Therefore, only the AgNW/Chi-AsA composite was further investigated. Unless otherwise specified, the AgNW composite in the following text is AgNW/Chi-AsA.
To determine the shape deformation among AgNWs on the substrate, field-emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM) images were recorded at a $30^{\circ}$ tilt. As shown in Fig. 3(a), the stacked pristine AgNWs showed upward-facing ends, as indicated by a red dashed line, and the crossed AgNWs exhibited no considerable shape deformation near the cross-junction point. This suggests the pristine AgNW networks were loosely stacked. In the AgNW composite, however, the downward-facing ends of the AgNWs are marked with a red dashed line, and a distinct conformal deformation is indicated by a red dashed circle, which occurred where AgNWs crossed, even between two closed cross junctions (Figs. 3(b) and 3(c)). Although the stacked pristine $\mathrm{\AgNWs}$ could deform and exhibited a trough near the middle of two distinct cross-junctions because of their good flexibility, conformal deformation occasionally occurred at crossjunctions when the spacing between AgNWs was very close, such as $\sim200\mathrm{nm}$ . Therefore, the difference between the morphology changes in the AgNW composite and the pristine $\mathtt{A g N W}$ network strongly suggest that the added Chi-AsA enabled tight contact between the AgNWs. This tight contact benefits a larger ohmic contact area between the intersecting AgNWs, thus enhancing conductivity and lowering surface roughness. As depicted in Figs. $3(\mathrm{d}){-}3(\mathrm{k}),$ , the RMS surface roughness of the $\mathsf{A g N W}$ composite coated with a $0.5~\mathrm{wt.\%}$ Chi-AsA solution was $7.7\mathrm{nm}$ over a scanning range of $10\upmu\mathrm{m}\times10\upmu\mathrm{m},$ while that of the pristine $\mathtt{A g N W}$ networks was $23.8\mathrm{nm}$ . This roughness can be further reduced to $4.3\mathrm{nm}$ by coating with a 1 wt. $\%$ Chi-AsA solution. Even when the scan size was as large as $50\upmu\mathrm{m}\times50\upmu\mathrm{m},$ the RMS was still as low as $6.7\mathrm{nm},$ , which was less than one-third of the RMS of the pristine $\mathtt{A g N W}$ networks scanned over $10\upmu\mathrm{m}\times10\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . To the best of our knowledge, this is one of the smoothest AgNW composite electrodes reported thus far.
The thickness of the top insulating layer significantly affects the surface roughness, conductivity, and stability of the AgNW composite electrode. Thus, we ablated a partial sample from the substrate using a needle and tested the step profile via atomic force microscopy (AFM). As shown in Figs. 4(a) and 4(b), the Chi-AsA layer in the gaps between AgNWs was $55.3\mathrm{nm}$ thick with the 1 wt. $\%$ Chi-AsA coating and only $20.3\mathrm{nm}$ thick with $0.5\mathrm{wt.\%}$ Chi-AsA. Generally, the Chi-AsA layer on top of the AgNWs was much thinner than the layer between AgNWs on the substrate. This means the $\operatorname{AgNW}$ networks were covered with a fairly thin layer of Chi-AsA, which is preferable for electrical applications because it minimizes the negative influence of the insulating coating on conductivity. However, such a thin layer would be disadvantageous to the stability and surface flatness of the $\operatorname{AgNW}$ composite, which are favored by a thick overcoating layer. As discussed above, our $\mathtt{A g N W}$ composite had a relatively flat surface even with the extremely thin Chi-AsA coating of $\sim20\mathrm{nm}$ . Subsequently, the conductivity and stability were thoroughly studied.
