where $\sigma_{\mathrm{Op}}(\lambda)$ is the optical conductivity, $\sigma_{\mathrm{DC}}$ is the direct current (DC) conductivity, $T$ is the transmittance, and $R_{s}$ is the sheet resistance of the film. A higher $\sigma_{\mathrm{DC}}/\sigma_{\mathrm{Op}}$ means better opto-electrical performance, which corresponds to lower sheet resistance and higher transmittance. AgNW composites coated with both 55.3 and $20.3\mathrm{nm}$ thick Chi-AsA layers showed lower sheet resistances than the pristine $\mathsf{A g N W}$ networks. In addition, the change in the sheet resistance due to the coating varied with the different samples, ranging from $-4.3\%$ to $-81.6\%$ . Furthermore, the plots of $\Delta R_{s}\%$ and $R_{\mathrm{so}}$ only showed scattered dots, suggesting that the change in the sheet resistance after Chi-AsA coating does not have a specific relationship with the conductivity of pristine AgNW networks. Even for the same pristine sheet resistance, for example, $\sim50\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}.$ , the change in the sheet resistance for the AgNW composites could be $13.8\%$ , $28.9\%$ and $48.5\%$ . However, when the change in the sheet resistance for the AgNW composite and $\sigma_{\mathrm{DC}}/\sigma_{\mathrm{Op}}$ of the pristine $\mathsf{A g N W}$ network were plotted, the data points lined up, demonstrating an inversely proportional linear relationship. Specifically, the change in sheet resistance was quite large $(>50\%)$ for the sample with a small pristine $\sigma_{\mathrm{DC}}/\sigma_{\mathrm{Op}}$ value $(<150)$ , but it became very small or zero $(<10\%)$ for a high pristine $\upsigma_{\mathrm{DC}}/\upsigma_{\mathrm{Op}}$ value $(\sim300)$ . In this case, it is helpful to minimize the conductivity fluctuations across the AgNW networks to obtain a more uniform electrode employing Chi-AsA combined with AgNWs. The relationship between the sheet resistance change for the $\mathtt{A g N W}$ composite and the pristine $\upsigma_{\mathrm{DC}}/\upsigma_{\mathrm{Op}}$ value might be involved in the deposition quality of random $\mathrm{\AgNWs}$ . Uniformly deposited AgNWs usually enable a high $\sigma_{\mathrm{DC}}/\sigma_{\mathrm{Op}}$ value, leading to more effective connections among random AgNWs. This makes the conductivity of pristine AgNW networks closer to the theoretical sheet resistance. Thus, when the additional treatment is applied, such a sample would show a smaller change in conductivity. solvent exposure and long-term stabilities of $\mathsf{A g N W}$ composite were investigated. Six chemicals were used for the solvent exposure tests including water, acetone, isopropyl alcohol (IPA), 2-methoxyethanol, methylbenzene, N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF). After $24\mathrm{h}$ of immersion, the AgNW composite exhibited good resistance to all the solvents without an obvious change in sheet resistance (Fig. 4(d)). Furthermore, pristine $\mathbf{AgNW}$ networks were immersed in the solvents for $24\mathrm{h}$ first and then coated with Chi-AsA. The samples were stored in ambient environment for 40 days, and the sheet resistance was recorded. The AgNW/Chi-AsA composites almost maintained their original sheet resistances during 40 days of storage in air, while the sheet resistance of the pristine $\mathsf{A g N W}$ networks exposed to solvent for $24\mathrm{h}$ increased by a factor of two or higher (Fig. 4(e)). This suggests that Chi-AsA dramatically improved the stability of AgNWs in the ambient environment. To further investigate the stability of $\mathrm{AgNW/Chi\mathrm{-}A s A},$ the sample was aged at a higher temperature of $85^{\circ}C$ in air (Fig. 4(f)). The AgNW composite coated with $55\mathrm{nm}$ of Chi-AsA maintained its initial sheet resistance of $17.1\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ even after 40 days. Moreover, the AgNW composite with only a $20\mathrm{-nm}$ -thick Chi-AsA coating exhibited very stable conductivity as well, as the sheet resistance only increased from 17.2 to $20.7\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ . Meanwhile, the sheet resistance of the pristine $\mathsf{A g N W}$ networks consistently increased from 20.7 to $94.5\Omega{\cdot}{\mathrm{sq}}^{-1}$ after 40 days of aging at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air. This indicates the good long-term stability of the $\mathtt{A g N W}$ composite electrode, outperforming those in almost all the other reports. This also represents significant progress compared to our previously reported AgNW composite with acetic-acid-modified chitosan, thus confirming the effectiveness of our new $\mathsf{A g N W}$ composite [43]. Notably, the long-term stability of pristine AgNWs varied among reports [24, 44–47]. We found the AgNWs from different resources exhibited differences in the stability performance. These differences can likely be attributed to impurities in the AgNW dispersion or the different AgNWs crystallization mechanisms with different synthesis methods, which affect the stability of pristine AgNWs during long-term aging [48]. More work is required to reveal the underlying mechanism. Nonetheless, the performances can still be compared since the AgNWs in both the pristine AgNW networks and the AgNW composite were from the same resource and underwent identical processing.
The stability of the strong adhesion between the AgNWs and the substrate is also important to enable thoroughly washing the electrode before device fabrication and easy handling during manufacturing. In this work, not only the common tape test but also harsher methods were used to evaluate the adhesion of the AgNW composite, namely, a scratch test and ultrasonication in a solvent. As shown in Figs. 5(a)– 5(d), AgNW composites with $\sim20$ and $\sim55\mathrm{nm}$ thick Chi-AsA coatings on rigid glass and flexible PEN substrates were investigated. Each tape test was conducted with a fresh piece of $19\mathrm{mm}$ adhesive tape (3M Scotch tape 600) for every single cycle for a total of 100 cycles (Fig. 5(a)). The sheet resistance and transmittance of all the AgNW composites did not obviously fluctuate and almost remained at their original values, whereas the sheet resistance of the pristine $\mathrm{\AgNWs}$ networks quadrupled after the first tape test. This indicates significant progress in resisting peeling off by tape for the Chi-AsA-coated AgNWs. Next, the sample surface was scratched with a glovecovered finger applying $50\mathrm{g}$ of force (Fig. 5(b)). The pristine AgNW lost its conductivity after experiencing only one stroke. In contrast, AgNW/1.0Chi-AsA on glass and PEN maintained their sheet resistances for 100 strokes. In addition, $\mathrm{AgNW}/0.5\mathrm{Chi-AsA},$ whose top layer was quite thin, maintained conductivity, and the sheet resistance only increased from $\sim15$ to $25{-}27\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ after 100 strokes. This suggests the AgNW composite is much more resistant to scratching. Finally, the sample was placed in water or acetone, and an ultrasonic shock with a power of $300\mathrm{W}$ and a frequency of $40\mathrm{kHz}$ was applied. The sheet resistances of all the $\mathtt{A g N W}$ composites were fixed at $\sim19\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ . After ultrasonication in water for as long as $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ (Fig. 5(c)), $\mathrm{AgNW/1.0Chi-AsA}$ showed a slightly increased sheet resistance of $\sim26~\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1},$ , and $\mathrm{\sfAgNW/}$ 0.5Chi-AsA also showed a relatively good sheet resistance of 33.5 and $39.8\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ on glass and PEN, respectively. The $\mathsf{A g N W}$ composite performed even better in acetone (Fig. 5(d)). After ultrasonication in acetone for up to $32\mathrm{h},$ the sheet resistance of AgNW/ 1.0Chi-AsA and AgNW/0.5Chi-AsA only increased to 27.5 and $\sim32.0\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ , respectively. However, the pristine $\mathsf{A g N W}$ lost its conductivity immediately in ultrasonication, and $\mathrm{\AgNWs}$ almost completely detached from the substrate within $1\mathrm{min}$ (Figs. 5(e) and 5(f)). In contrast, AgNWs in the composite stayed on substrate without visible damage during $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ of ultrasonication in acetone (Fig. $5(\mathrm{g})$ ) and were still located in their original positions $32\mathrm{h}$ later (Fig. $5(\mathrm{h})\dot{}$ ). A few disconnections appeared along the AgNWs in the AgNW composite, which increased in number with longer ultrasonication times, thus accounting for the increase in sheet resistance. The stability of such composite films has rarely been tested using ultrasonication in the reported research, and most AgNW composite electrodes have failed this test. In contrast, our AgNW composite exhibited good durability.
Moreover, the thermal stability of the AgNW composite was studied by placing the sample on a hotplate to undergo aging at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. S2 in the ESM). The sheet resistance of the pristine $\mathsf{A g N W}$ networks sharply increased from $\sim20\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ to thousands of Ohms per square in less than $10\mathrm{{h}}$ of aging, whereas the $\mathsf{A g N W}$ composites on glass and PEN showed a slight increase in sheet resistance by $15\%{-}20\%$ after $10\mathrm{{h}}$ of aging and still exhibited conductivity after $32\mathrm{h}$ . Interestingly, although the selected aging temperature of $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ exceeds the glass transition temperature of the PEN substrate, the $\mathsf{A g N W}$ composite on PEN maintained its conductivity as well as that on glass. This might be because the top-coated Chi-AsA secured the $\mathrm{\DeltaAgNWs}$ and limited their movement along with the heat-induced deformation of the substrate. Furthermore, the crosslinking points of the embedded AgNWs secured by the top Chi-AsA layer were also prevented from deforming, and the diffusion of surface $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ atoms became slower than those in the pristine AgNW network. Therefore, the ripening effect, which results in the breakdown of crossed AgNWs, was postponed. This may explain why the coating on the $\mathsf{A g N W}$ composite electrode improved the thermal stability of the AgNW network.
Considering the good surface flatness, conductivity, stability, and adhesion, the $\mathtt{A g N W}$ composite was employed in $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ based perovskite solar cells as the bottom electrode after a thorough washing. The solar cells were fabricated as described in the Experimental section. PEDOT:PSS was used as a buffer layer and $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ as the active layer. Al/Bphen was deposited on top as the cathode, and the photovoltaic performance was characterized, as shown in Fig. 6. The photo current-density–voltage $(J{-}V)$ curve suggested a short circuit current of $21.0\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ , an open circuit voltage of $1.04\mathrm{V},$ and fill factor of $36.1\%,$ , resulting in a high PCE of $7.9\%$ for the best device. This PCE is nearly $75\%$ of that of the reference device with the ITO electrode (Fig. S3 in the ESM) [49], and it is better than or comparable to that of the perovskite solar cell with AgNW/metal oxide as the bottom electrode. In contrast, our AgNW composite electrode requires a much easier fabrication process [27, 28]. For a clear comparison, the performances of the AgNW composite electrodes from this work and the reported research are listed in Table 1. Our AgNW composites show advantages in their long-term stability, overall performance, easy fabrication process, and device application [23, 24, 27, 28, 36, 39, 43, 47, 50–53].
## 3 Conclusion
We developed a novel AgNW composite electrode with antioxidant-acid-modified chitosan using a lowtemperature solution process. It exhibited a superior flat surface with a RMS surface roughness of less than $10\mathrm{nm}$ over a large scan size of $50\upmu\mathrm{m}\times50\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . It also demonstrated good long-term stability without an obvious change in the sheet resistance over 40 days of $85^{\circ}C$ aging in air. Moreover, it strongly adhered to the substrate, and this adhesion was stable against ultrasonication attack both in water and organic solvent for hours. Furthermore, the conductivity of composite electrode was also enhanced rather than sacrificed by the polymer coating, which improved the electrode’s stability, adhesion and flatness. A composite electrode with comparable figures of merit $\left(\boldsymbol{R}_{\mathrm{s}}\sim18\boldsymbol{\Omega}{\cdot}\mathbf{s}\mathbf{q}^{-1},\right.$ $T\sim93.5\%,$ ) to that of commercial ITO $(\sigma_{\mathrm{DC}}/\sigma_{\mathrm{Op}}\sim350)$ ) was obtained and employed in a traditional perovskite solar cell, resulting in a PCE of $7.9\%$ , which is nearly $75\%$ of the PCE of the reference solar cell on patterned ITO.
## 4 Experimental
## 4.1 Materials
AgNWs with a diameter of $\sim30\mathrm{nm}$ and length of $\sim15\upmu\mathrm{m}$ were bought from Zhejiang Kechuang Advanced Materials $C0$ ., Ltd., and they were dispersed in IPA. Chitosan ( $100{-}200\mathrm{mPas})$ was purchased from Energy Chemical. The deacetylation ratio was calculated to be $94.6\%$ according to a reported method via nuclear magnetic resonance investigations, as shown in Fig. S4 (in the ESM) [54]. All the chemicals for fabricating the perovskite solar cells were purchased from Shanghai MaterWin New Materials Co., Ltd. and used as received. The phenyl-C61-butyric acid methyl ester (PC61BM) was purchased from Nano-C, Inc.
## 4.2 Synthesis of chitosan solution
Since pristine chitosan powder is not soluble in water, acids including ascorbic acid, citric acid, and tartaric acid were added to form $1\mathrm{wt.\%}$ and $0.5\mathrm{wt.\%}$ chitosan aqueous solutions, followed by agitating by hand and sonication for $10\mathrm{min}$ . All the solutions were stored for $24\mathrm{h}$ before use, enabling complete dissolution.
## 4.3 Synthesis of AgNW/Chi composite electrode
The glass and PEN substrates were washed with acetone, ethanol, and deionized water. The $\mathtt{A g N W}$ suspension was rod-coated onto clean PEN or glass after dilution to $1.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{\cdotmL}^{-1}$ . Then, the prepared chitosan solution was spin-coated onto the AgNW networks at $3,000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $60\mathrm{{s}}$ to form AgNW/Chi composite, followed by drying at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ . Samples were labeled AgNW/Chi-AsA, AgNW/Chi-CiA, and AgNW/Chi-TA according to their respective antioxidant additives. For the $0.5\mathrm{wt.\%}$ and $1\mathrm{wt.\%}$ chitosan solutions with AsA, the final composites were labeled AgNW/0.5Chi-AsA and AgNW/1.0Chi-AsA, respectively.
## 4.4 Stability tests
To test the thermal stability, each sample was kept on a hotplate at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to monitor the resistance change. To test their stability in solvents, samples were separately immersed in water, acetone, IPA, 2-methoxyethanol, methylbenzene, and DMF for $24\mathrm{h}$ . For the long-term stability test, the pristine $\mathsf{A g N W}$ networks were first separately immersed in acetone, water, and IPA for $24\mathrm{h}$ and then coated with chitosan and stored in ambient environment for 40 days to observe changes in the sheet resistance. For the accelerated long-term stability test, AgNW/Chi was kept in an oven at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 40 days, and the change in the sheet resistance was tracked. To investigate the adhesion stability, a $90^{\circ}$ tape test was carried out on the sample using $19\mathrm{mm}$ adhesive tape (Scotch tape 600, 3M) and the sheet resistance and optical transmittance were recorded during the tape test. In addition, samples were sonicated in water and acetone to further evaluate their stability.
## 4.5 Perovskite solar cell preparation
The $\mathrm{AgNW}/0.5\mathrm{Chi-AsA}$ composite with a sheet resistance of $18\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1}$ and a transmittance of $93.5\%$ and commercial ITO were cleaned via immersion in acetone, $\mathrm{IPA},$ and water for 10 min each, followed by drying at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ . Then, they were treated in an ultraviolet (UV) ozone cleaner for $20\mathrm{min}$ to enhance the hydrophilicity. Filtrated semiconducting PEDOT:PSS (CleviosTM P VP AI 4083, Heraeus) was spin-coated onto the AgNW composite electrode at $3,000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $60\:s.$ . The sample was then annealed in air at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ . The active perovskite layer was fabricated according to a procedure in the literature. $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ were dissolved in DMF at a molar ratio of 1:1 to a final concentration of $1.25\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ .
The resulting solution was spin-cast at $5,000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{{s}}$ while dropping $\mathrm{\sim0.1\mL}$ of methylbenzene during spin-coating. The active layer was further annealed thermally at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min},$ resulting in a $250\mathrm{-nm}$ -thick layer. Next, a $20\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{\cdotmL}^{-1}$ PCBM solution in chlorobenzene was spin-coated on top of the formed perovskite layer. A $3\mathrm{nm}$ thick Bphen layer and an $80\mathrm{nm}$ thick Al layer were then consecutively deposited via thermal evaporation at $<10^{-5}$ Pa to complete the device fabrication. The active area was defined by a shadow mask to be $4\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ .
## 4.6 Characterization
Optical transmittance spectra were recording using a T6 New Century UV spectrophotometer. SEM was carried out on a Zeiss Ultra 55 FE-SEM with an SE2 detector. AFM was performed on a Bruker MultiMode 8 scanning probe microscope in tapping mode. The sheet resistance measured by a four-point probe from Shanghai Qianfeng Electronic Instrument Co., Ltd. The $J{-}V$ characteristics were measured with a Keithley 2400 semiconductor characterization system. The photovoltaic performance was determined under air mass 1.5 solar illumination at $100\mathrm{mW}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (1 sun).
